    "C38","C44","C46",
    "C48","C49","C50","C51","C54","C56","C57","C58"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "All visible L1 gold records in human or clinically relevant populations report absolute oral bioavailability at or above 24%, including aminoglutethimide ≈100% (C03), molsidomine 44–59% (C04), maraviroc 24% at 100 mg (C12), and amoxicillin 70–89% (C01, C05, C08); the only visible sub-20% record is ampicillin 12% in fasted veal calves (C17), a low-transferability species-specific result, while the same calf model gives amoxicillin 32% (C23).",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C03","C04","C05","C08","C12","C17","C23"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The most transferable small basic-amine contexts—aminoglutethimide, molsidomine, and maraviroc—all exceed the 20% threshold, supporting that small ionizable amines can achieve F ≥ 20% despite ionization.",
      "card_ids": ["C03","C04","C12","C15","C21"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral-exposure records for saxagliptin, an adamantane-containing small molecule, show measurable oral AUC (AUCinf about 145–153 ng·h/mL) after single oral doses, supporting oral absorption of this structural class, though no IV comparator or absolute F is reported.",
      "card_ids": ["C24","C25","C26","C27","C30","C31","C32","C33","C34","C35"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 Caco-2 records classify small basic amines norephedrine and cathine as highly permeable (Papp >1×10^-6 cm/s; norephedrine 10.0×10^-6 cm/s in a mixture), supporting that small ionized primary amines can still be well absorbed; this is analog evidence, not a direct query measurement.",
      "card_ids": ["C38","C44","C46"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 Caco-2 bidirectional transport records show that small basic amines (dexamphetamine, p-methoxyephedrine) have high A-B permeability and are not P-gp substrates (efflux ratios 0.9–1.2; no P-gp-mediated efflux with PSC-833/GF120918), and leucine is absorbed via an intestinal carrier; this supports absence of gut-wall efflux as a barrier for the query's structural class, but none of these records measure rimantadine itself.",
      "card_ids": ["C48","C49","C50","C51","C54","C56","C57","C58"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records are for other molecules (BDB, MDA, imatinib cage analogs, 2-aminobutanol, menthol, isopropylamine, mexiletine, terpinen-4-ol, and an adamantane-containing benzamide) and provide no direct measurement of rimantadine first-pass loss; C65 weakly supports cage-related metabolic stability, while C71 is a high-clearance rat-hepatocyte result for a structurally dissimilar adamantane-containing compound and is not threshold-relevant for the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C60","C61","C62","C63","C64","C65","C66","C67","C68","C69","C70","C71"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No visible record provides affirmative threshold-relevant evidence of F < 20% for the query; the only sub-20% record (C17) is a low-transferability calf result for ampicillin.",
      "card_ids": ["C17"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C60","C61","C62","C63","C64","C65","C66","C67","C68","C69","C70","C71"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct oral bioavailability or matched oral-versus-IV exposure measurement for rimantadine itself.",
    "No direct permeability, solubility, gut-wall efflux, or intestinal metabolism measurement for rimantadine; L4/L5 records are for amphetamine analogs, Khat alkaloids, leucine, and unrelated molecules.",
    "No direct hepatic clearance or metabolic stability measurement for rimantadine; L6 records are for other molecules and do not establish first-pass loss.",
    "Full ionization at pH 7.4 (logD -0.82) leaves some permeability uncertainty, though small size and the lipophilic cage may compensate.",
    "Transfer from record contexts is based on structural analogy, not identical chemistry."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F ≥ 20%) because all visible L1 gold records in relevant populations are positive (≥24%), the most transferable small basic-amine contexts support adequate oral bioavailability, L3 oral-exposure records add supportive but non-quantitative context, L4 Caco-2 permeability of similar small basic amines supports absorption, L5 shows no P-gp efflux for similar small basic amines, and L6 provides no affirmative threshold-relevant evidence of high first-pass loss for rimantadine. Confidence remains moderate because no direct query F measurement exists and the query is fully ionized at pH 7.4."
}

